  "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards drawn until a star card is obtained. The chance of selecting a star card from the deck is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of selections before obtaining a star card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nSo, $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{5}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1-1}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2-1}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3-1}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =[1+(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}](\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =[1+\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{9}{25}](\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =0.784.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a star card after the third draw is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.784 \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
